{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-162383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/5907cf8a9103cc9a639fd8078da3646cc0395d28827f58c0f1beef0a1c06645b.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a89646433c8851f66c94092b75fae7b46b01f31c69ca03d338464b87bd7f5df4.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le y}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=y\\,⟹\\,y=x\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x\\le y\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1604e44050208cfb3b4dd81d35bff9769c7498d8304583d5ba92ad4b2f62fd05.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162383" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162383" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c420d45ca9418fd29889d91a49cce4ae80bb71bdfd4e4aa701b495cc8d87335f.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8b7cfa72ed3dbfb121039c6e64b843d83489878a9f457fb1386e455e7d9649ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/f49af5da2e4f1f281700e16c4659fabfcd0b15661947ca985d38f208f8563877.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+y}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162226" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+y}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/024df0fe0a965feba2177adfac8bbd658fc90f4775a4707f13eba1ef2cb94702.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z},\\,\\,1+y-z\\le x\\le 2}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8fff232443e6da697862c2e85a9d3abfe0554b80c2fcbfa8c93de42b94a45691.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt[3]{z}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt[3]{z}\\,⟹\\,z={y}^{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,{y}^{3}\\le z\\le 1},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/726ff420cae25c40f212714141a134fab84633cca7f49bdd268f0668d5248766.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{1+y}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c72489a2d33a181fe7bc07d5cc017b93daccac51889cbb61edd64d4ee34429df.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/98aa4c0a436e4649ccd3d48a53fbf94b0341bf30b95745c4d052381680d223cf.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/47380c108c5b271761de2a027399bbd5981f2a9bdff21e498d73d0b430d070c7.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162376" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}+1}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/fcc776a63cd1ac9fc5ec91157652ce9e75c6ee515eca8c6223309521cc300b61.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{-4/x}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 2+\\frac{xy}{2}}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1f30c28c7c048faae864da08a5a6d67d0c6346b27c00a9e7726234531f2f0783.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 4,\\,\\,-\\frac{4}{x}\\le y\\le -1}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{x}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nand the left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le y\\le -1,\\,\\,1\\le x\\le -\\frac{4}{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/acc210f36bccb873526172149b85941bd42bc1648a464862562723fedca27bcb.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-4/y}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{-4/y}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4/(xy)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{0}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4/(xy)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{\\int }_{1}^{-4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{2+xy/2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}-2}^{y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-3}^{p}{\\int }_{-1}^{q}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{r}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,y)}^{{u}_{4}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}-2\\le x\\le y,\\,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le z\\le x+4}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{y}-2}^{y}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(x)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c92ab09dc71043ac3f5265202f36e38110e5e8db9f2dd115ab006721ed66d4c0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt[3]{y}-2\\le x\\le y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x,$ we split $D$ into two type I regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/8ac0e7631d0baa8ba3b4c37ebe2b2b1d8efa650495d197f425aba093f342a9d3.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the left region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=-1\\,$ and $\\,y=(x+2)^{3}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the right region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=x\\,$ and $\\,y=1.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-1\\le y\\le (x+2)^{3}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,x\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}{\\int }_{-1}^{(x+2)^{3}}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{x}^{1}{\\int }_{y/2}^{x+4}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r=x"] ["math" "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r={x}^{2}"] ["math" "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt[3]{x+2},\\,\\,\\,r=x"] ["math" "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=3(x+2),\\,\\,\\,r=-x"] ["math" "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt[3]{x+2},\\,\\,\\,r=-x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,\\,r=x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{-1}^{p}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{q}{\\int }_{0}^{r}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(xy,z)}^{{u}_{4}(y,z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2},\\,\\,-2z\\le x\\le y+3}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}-1}^{2-y/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(z)}^{{v}_{4}(x)}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a4de9d9df51efcec67253a1c7118466f29459ffd5c9136c144d33500ec5557bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2y}-1\\le z\\le 2-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/18bb3e55f076e022176bb60df27d5665ed2d91d75d618969c04ce192378db922.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $y=0\\,$ and $\\,y=4-2z.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}(z+1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-2z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{(z+1)^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-2z}{\\int }_{-2z}^{y+3}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r=z-2"] ["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z"] ["math" "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4"] ["math" "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=1,\\,\\,\\,r={z}^{2}-4"] ["math" "p=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\,\\,q=3,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162332" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p=1,\\,\\,\\,q=2,\\,\\,\\,r=4-2z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration, find the missing limits in the following repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{p}^{0}{\\int }_{q}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{r}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{3}(x,y)}^{{u}_{4}(x,y)}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3},\\,\\,x\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{({x}^{2}-4)/2}^{(x+2)^{3}/4}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{c}^{d}{\\int }_{{v}_{3}(y)}^{{v}_{4}(y)}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/a38afc63496b487803590ccd57e2ab92ad4061524007e8bb4fb6277ff952dde2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4)\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{4}(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we split $D$ into two type II regions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/9bbaf6d9ce11ecbd988954365e9693f7f3f175c1dd0f944cc85b73773beca047.png)\n\nNotice that the bottom and top boundaries of the lower region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nAlso, the bottom and top boundaries of the upper region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ are\n\n$\\,$ $x=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\,$ and $\\,x=0.$\n\nSo, we can write $D$ as a union of\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 0,\\,-\\sqrt{2y+4}\\le x\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2\\le x\\le 0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{2y+4}}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2}^{0}{\\int }_{x}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{y+1},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2"] ["math" "p=-1,\\,\\,\\,q=\\sqrt{y+1},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{y}"] ["math" "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2"] ["math" "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{y}"] ["math" "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y}+1,\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{2y}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162333" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "p=-2,\\,\\,\\,q=-\\sqrt{2y+4},\\,\\,\\,r=\\sqrt[3]{4y}-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y+2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{2}^{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{6-y}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le y\\le 6,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y},\\,\\,-z\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/aa1c884900c3cb01a719dc6d22604469984d2b08291d9d925b8f37d794d455b2.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{y+2}}\\,\\cup \\,{(y,z)\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 6,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{6-y}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{y+2}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=\\sqrt{6-y}\\,⟹\\,y=6-{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,{z}^{2}-2\\le y\\le 6-{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/e4139dde4d56e83b91e4a72d42ccdf296ad20773520ccdaa51d7a84b9e1a3f31.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{2{z}^{2}}^{6+{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{z-2}^{6-z}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}+1}^{6-\\sqrt{z}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162249" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{z}^{2}-2}^{6-{z}^{2}}{\\int }_{-z}^{{y}^{2}}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $x,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-2{x}^{3}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2x}}^{2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,-y\\le z\\le x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/834238127fb9a55c956b2dd708e1dc7d925c63725990003eff6aac28ee42a873.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type I regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-2{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 2}\\,\\cup \\,{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\sqrt{2x}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=-2{x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{2x}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,-\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{y}{2}}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/82bddc0c83e0e5a5824b7525bcd4fd2dc9b8f88473dfe1fd5c6cdb2ff0cefcaf.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y}}^{2{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{2y}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2y}}^{{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162247" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-\\sqrt[3]{y/2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}{\\int }_{-y}^{x+3}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,\\,0\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/62377f3e744c5767927f9591b8c6e3335a8036cad5fe9c62f52c041b2db2bac8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=x\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi }{2}},\\,0\\le x\\le y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/05de07930f6cacfd0a3a21045a95628891965bb69d5df1bef4dbeeab9e480b97.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})[x]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡({y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({y}^{2})]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}"] ["math" "\\pi "] ["math" "{\\pi }^{2}-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162251" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{3}y}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/14c67512e143995cbfcfcf9e80c8d0975bbbc42368c97aa29956bd2d3f3454a8.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le e,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\le y\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=1\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le {e}^{y}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/d18470b45d21b4734187c1f639784a4e75924ed212e3fa4c7a214d19eaeb6022.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡z}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}y}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{3}y}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{\\pi }{3}{\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}[z]_{1}^{{e}^{y}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi }{3}({e}^{y}-1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}[{e}^{y}-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(e-1-1+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "4e"] ["math" "\\frac{2\\pi e}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}"] ["math" "\\pi e+1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162334" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi (e-2)}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y,\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c5f4ec9b9b12411f3057bc074a14a1f541c768c30d3a9746d7a0484b0491ad2e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/077fc8133673cd57aba570cca9a6e0f5f25aa742720948c46db2b9e1d05add69.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡y}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}y\\,[x]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}2y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[{y}^{2}]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2z)\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[z+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2z]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{4\\pi -1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162330" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {e}^{z/x}}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/e83c249a6abf9710a201462e16c646a11411dbcdbca46f34e9beb8d2db7a654b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{z}\\le x\\le 1}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{z}\\,⟹\\,z={x}^{2}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le {x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/7e684766b85a214d1f1dd7845979052c532e11ecce74d0284b8fc379b5669957.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}[y]_{0}^{{e}^{z/x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}{e}^{z/x}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[x{e}^{z/x}]_{z=0}^{z={x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x{e}^{x}-x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[(x-1){e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})-(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}-1"] ["math" "e-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162253" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2yz}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/d3865f790df8c2a228f08c290a113954687ade63a275a754c4cb3199c234d5ec.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z\\,⟹\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD={(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,0\\le z\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡2y},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/085b2ce5a9ecd2632f70f287bbef5e9f04566871fc8adf7ba10076e2e875ca2e.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡z)/2}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}[{\\int }_{0}^{2yz}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}{[-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{z})]}_{x=0}^{x=2yz}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}(-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y+z)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}[(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}z\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){[\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y){sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,\\text{d}y-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2y\\,{sin}^{2}⁡2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[y-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y]}_{0}^{\\pi /4}-\\frac{1}{12}[{sin}^{3}⁡2y]_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0)-\\frac{1}{12}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "36dd0da4b97318463e1d00b35f592790638f84b2270ccdbb6b1921edd4e66c71"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\pi -3"] ["math" "24\\pi "] ["math" "4(2\\pi +1)"] ["math" "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi +1}{12}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "q-162331" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi -4}{48}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/state.json" "file_sha256" "129e42c395daba36e405220175ae269af7e0234d7d65cf4b1ca10a4d581c1409"} {"question" "e-12401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/5a19eefe8fdcd63ef9f8a35bf4517b022ab2c35b5fccd06e1e797f67e567653a.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_0^1\\int_{\\sqrt{z}}^1\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12401.json" "file_sha256" "0eb7c1963c1a7cc7c9ad8784ecf45a2d131ebb568f1825d1293db95cd540e491"} {"question" "e-12401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/ec195fe4aa1bec5221ec25a1921dfe36e78db34ad3e295fec541c1d05a85d60d.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{z}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nz = y^2\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z\\leq y^2 \\big\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/841f014cae7bb224b6f743a86583cb0a70a423d60176b2efd6482d155aa020bc.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V\n=\n\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{0}^{y^2} \\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12401.json" "file_sha256" "0eb7c1963c1a7cc7c9ad8784ecf45a2d131ebb568f1825d1293db95cd540e491"} {"question" "e-12403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z,$ then $y,$ and then $x,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\n\\int_{-1}^0 \\int_{y^2}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n+\n\\int_0^1 \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12403.json" "file_sha256" "86c7f8c1dcff7cd242cedd9bef2b62753edff5cf9b2e29c4b8ca8b10f7f1c1fe"} {"question" "e-12403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)}  f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R = R_1\\cup R_2,$ where $R_1$ and $R_2$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 0,\\,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,y\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,x-1\\le z\\le {y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c8bfd933472e1944f63b1295bda7fc324311ea2ca2ada3636e9f5ab82e4e5fc5.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD =\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad y^2 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n\\:\\cup\\:\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the lower boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx=y^2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny = -\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the upper boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=y\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\ny =x.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad -\\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq  x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/c73a0e30d6996490f9f41bf3e8777c14d542755387f643ad464bd1dd6cab2839.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-\\sqrt{x}}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x}} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12403.json" "file_sha256" "86c7f8c1dcff7cd242cedd9bef2b62753edff5cf9b2e29c4b8ca8b10f7f1c1fe"} {"question" "e-12404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle\n\\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12404.json" "file_sha256" "0c316a89844a1ea586046ba832af9ed436c829dc2d5a2bca694047f5bc64480c"} {"question" "e-12404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: y \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z,$ the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/bdd856ef8a7940a009b7ee27d6614fdb8c042e6655c2316ce4db0633bfd04e9f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y = y(x),$ is\n\n$$\nx = y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny = 0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/assets/1e28e1cef9743da80d6fd2d38840810b12239d8546254c4d4cca767568f76a53.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{\\int }_{0}^{y}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[z]_{0}^{y}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3y{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{e}^{{x}^{3}}{[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[{e}^{{x}^{3}}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/example-12404.json" "file_sha256" "0c316a89844a1ea586046ba832af9ed436c829dc2d5a2bca694047f5bc64480c"} {"question" "q-162527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "ea88ab4c9f6fd107bb766c7ee8657e080156cb2598c3e5deed45bac24739d418" "reviewer_session" "01a11908-fa8d-7c61-8e03-c1185d0b9506" "rationale" "The original projection satisfies 0≤z≤1 and 0≤y≤∛z. Since cubing is increasing, this is equivalently 0≤y≤1 and y³≤z≤1. Integration with respect to x remains first, so its bounds remain 1+y-z≤x≤2. This yields original option 0 exactly, matching the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. This is a derived mathematical judgment. The malformed stored 'y 3' should not be globally equated with y³." "bindings" {"problem" "d8565a81817c5081af949647be5cd36a3d32f2051223bc70c199d610a94fbd4d" "worked_solution" "0012624b8bdd68bc4c2a5b3eedd9cecb9ffa7ccc8be2b67d8ad336f470535250" "original_field" "2f714b3c5ef96fc5d72be699f6007e707aaf002cbee4a56351ba465009e9c662" "reviewed_field" "e3e100f0b3726452fda279baffeeadf144c969b526b107ce893d90c9f13fd2f5" "raw_widget" "5c973b839cb1d927d6cfe4abd057bfbc6bfd1b3ceb9f87618cea79be5530160e"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-162527-before.png" "sha256" "0cb8ac3b4a663e199e52b045262c89ff666d85e43d493208d3919bcdc8576557"} {"path" "history-q-162527.png" "sha256" "db9e0ac0ce1e25f44db28f700518ddcd39dcbbe3efe8a7f88170c19f6ddcb61c"} {"path" "q-162527-after.json" "sha256" "72fdddb14044ee4a7da666ba51ebc3a5f11c1df7c9b1fb938e61f62230c9f3a5"} {"path" "edb-import/questions.edn" "sha256" "d3804f57f1c120ff92e8a2be6666e290248e204c04f226c0a03b10d31912cf92"}]} {"question" "q-162527" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{1+y}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{1+y-z}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{3}}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt[3]{1+y-z}}^{2}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072974/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "ea88ab4c9f6fd107bb766c7ee8657e080156cb2598c3e5deed45bac24739d418" "rationale" "The original projection satisfies 0≤z≤1 and 0≤y≤∛z. Since cubing is increasing, this is equivalently 0≤y≤1 and y³≤z≤1. Integration with respect to x remains first, so its bounds remain 1+y-z≤x≤2. This yields original option 0 exactly, matching the authentic worked solution and saved Correct grading. This is a derived mathematical judgment. The malformed stored 'y 3' should not be globally equated with y³."}] "usage" {"e-12401" [] "e-12404" [] "q-162253" [] "q-162527" [] "e-12403" [] "q-162226" []} "basis" 2664 "retractions" [[17592186129463 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/q-162226.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x, y, z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x^{2}}∫_{0}^{x^{2} + y}f(x, y, z)dzdydx$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $z$, then $x$, and then $y$."] [17592186129465 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/q-162527.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x, y, z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$∭_(R)f(x, y, z)dV = ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt[3]{z}}∫_{1 + y - z}^{2}f(x, y, z)dxdydz$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x$, then $z$, and then $y$."] [17592186129465 :question/answer-fields 17592186126596] [17592186129464 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$∫_{0}^{1}∫_{\\sqrt{z}}^{1}∫_{0}^{e^{z/x}}dydxdz$.\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $dydzdx$.*"] [17592186128790 :question/problem "The triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/d9125842cf8cd48196ce9ab2ae311a32.png)\n\n$\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_0^1\\int_{\\sqrt{z}}^1\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x$, then $z$, and then $y$."] [17592186128790 :question/worked-solution "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq y^2-z \\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_{u_1(y,z)}^{u_2(y,z)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x$, the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_a^b\\int_{v_1(y)}^{v_2(y)}\\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/1de21d0b8286065d87483c152533f036.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq z \\leq 1, \\:\\: \\sqrt{z} \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}z$ to $\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y)$, is\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{z} \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad z = y^2\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=0\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z\\leq y^2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/a9c473cff1bba6666cf90eca138e4e13.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}V = \\int_0^1 \\int_{0}^{y^2} \\int_0^{y^2-z} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\n$$"] [17592186128792 :question/problem "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $z$, then $y$, and then $x$, express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 \\int_{y^2}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y + \\int_0^1 \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$"] [17592186128792 :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n$\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$.\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration\n\n$$\nR = R_1\\cup R_2\n$$\n\nwhere $R_1$ and $R_2$ are type I regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_{1} &= {(x, y, z): - 1 \\le y \\le 0, y^{2} \\le x \\le 1, x - 1 \\le z \\le y^{2}}, \\\\\nR_{2} &= {(x, y, z): 0 \\le y \\le 1, y \\le x \\le 1, x - 1 \\le z \\le y^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z$, the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/8a2a53019675f847983cf091720b77e6.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 0, \\quad y^2 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\} \\:\\cup\\: \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}x\\:\\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the lower boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx=y^2 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y = -\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nand the upper boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nx=y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y =x\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad -\\sqrt{x} \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/2a5803a3e93d07a1a44e35de17918573.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1}} \\int_{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{-\\sqrt{x}}}^{\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{x}} \\int_{x-1}^{y^2} f(x,y,z) \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$"] [17592186128793 :question/problem "Evaluate\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\int_{y}^{1} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\: \\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$.\n\n*Hint: Change change the order of integration to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$.*"] [17592186128793 :question/worked-solution "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n$\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{u_1(x,y)}^{u_2(x,y)} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$.\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nR = \\left\\{(x,y,z) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\:\\: y \\leq x \\leq 1, \\:\\: 0 \\leq z \\leq y \\right\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $z$, the $z$-limits remain the same as before:\n$\\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{v_1(x)}^{v_2(x)} \\int_{0}^{y} 3e^{x^3} \\:\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/bb0a1978ac6000ee6b166d46b4d5a7b0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type II region in the $xy$-plane:\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad y \\leq x \\leq 1 \\right\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}x \\: \\textrm{d}y$ to $\\textrm{d}z \\: \\textrm{d}y \\: \\textrm{d}x$, we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $y = y(x)$, is\n\n$$\nx = y \\qquad\\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = x\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region $D$ is\n$y = 0$.\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y) \\::\\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\quad 0 \\leq y \\leq x \\right\\}\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2056/Source/Images/9e35eedb89fe4cd2e1aca34b540f9d85.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{1}∫_{y}^{1}∫_{0}^{y}3e^{x^{3}}dzdxdy &= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}∫_{0}^{y}3e^{x^{3}}dzdydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3e^{x^{3}}[∫_{0}^{y}dz]dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3e^{x^{3}}[z]_{0}^{y}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}∫_{0}^{x}3ye^{x^{3}}dydx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3e^{x^{3}}[∫_{0}^{x}ydy]dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}3e^{x^{3}}[\\frac{1}{2}y^{2}]_{0}^{x}dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{2}x^{2}e^{x^{3}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}[e^{x^{3}}]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186126585 17592186126586 17592186126587 17592186126588 17592186126589 17592186126591 17592186126592 17592186126593 17592186126594 17592186126595 17592186126597 17592186126598 17592186126599 17592186126600 17592186126601] "immutable_fields" [[17592186126584 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126584 :answer-field/id] [17592186126584 :answer-field/key] [17592186126584 :answer-field/type] [17592186126590 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126590 :answer-field/id] [17592186126590 :answer-field/key] [17592186126590 :answer-field/type] [17592186126596 :answer-field/correct] [17592186126596 :answer-field/id] [17592186126596 :answer-field/key]